c/boot/efi/acpi-mb2-tag.patch -- teach acpi.c to take the RSDP from the
Multiboot2 information block when the loader supplied one.

STATUS: APPLIED at integration, 2026-08-15 -- acpi.c/acpi.h/kmain.c now carry
this change (plus one line the patch missed: kmain.c needed #include "acpi.h",
which check-acpi-patch.sh could not catch because it compile-tested only
acpi.c). Kept as the record of WHY; do not apply again.

DELIVERED AS A PATCH FILE AND NOT AS AN EDIT, ON PURPOSE. c/kernel/cpu/acpi.c
is owned by another line of work right now (the power line is editing it), and
this milestone's rule is that the kernel does not change. So the change lives
here, with its reasoning, for the orchestrator to apply at integration.

================================ THE PROBLEM =================================

acpi.c finds the RSDP by scanning physical 0xE0000-0xFFFFF on 16-byte
boundaries (find_rsdp(), acpi.c:62-72). That is the BIOS mechanism, and it is
the ONLY mechanism acpi.c has.

Under UEFI that area holds nothing. There is no EBDA and no BIOS ROM: the RSDP
is published through the EFI configuration table (UEFI 2.10 sec 4.6) and
nowhere else. Measured on the machine the UEFI gate runs on, the RSDP sits at
0x1fb7e014 -- inside EfiACPIReclaimMemory near the top of RAM, half a gigabyte
away from anywhere a scan would look.

So today the first UEFI boot comes up with no ACPI at all: no MADT, hence one
CPU and no SMP; no MCFG, hence no PCIe ECAM. That is ACCEPTED for the UEFI
milestone -- tests/boot/run-uefi-test.sh asserts "[acpi] no RSDP" is PRESENT,
so that when this patch lands the assertion breaks on purpose and whoever lands
it has to update the harness to expect SMP rather than quietly leaving a
regained capability unchecked.

================================ THE FIX =====================================

The loader ALREADY forwards the RSDP. c/boot/efi/loader.c's build_acpi_tag()
walks the configuration table, prefers the ACPI 2.0 GUID over the 1.0 one,
validates the "RSD PTR " signature, and copies the RSDP verbatim into the
information block as Multiboot2 tag type 15 (or type 14 for a v1 RSDP,
Multiboot2 spec sec 3.6.14/3.6.15). Verified on the gate:

    [efi] rsdp acpi2 0x1fb7e014 len 36 -> mb2 tag 15

A COPY, not a pointer -- the firmware's original lives in
EfiACPIReclaimMemory, which is by definition memory the kernel may reuse.

So all acpi.c has to do is look at the tag first and fall back to the scan.
Note this is not UEFI-specific plumbing: GRUB emits the same tags on BIOS, so
after this patch the tag is the primary path on BOTH loaders and the scan
becomes the fallback it always should have been.

WHY THE TAG MUST WIN WHEN BOTH EXIST: on a machine with a CSM the scan can
find a stale or shadowed copy, while the tag is what the firmware itself
published. Preferring the tag is preferring the authority.

================================ PLUMBING ====================================

acpi.c cannot reach the information block today: acpi_tables_init() is called
lazily (from acpi_find_table() and acpi_init(), acpi.c:138 and :184), long
after kernel_main's `mb_info` argument has gone out of scope. Hence the two-line
hunk in kmain.c that hands the address over before anything can ask for a table.

That setter is the whole cost. There is deliberately no attempt to parse the
tag list in kmain and pass a struct: pmm.c and fb.c each walk the tag list
themselves for the tag they want, and this follows that existing shape rather
than inventing a third.

================================ APPLYING ====================================

Line numbers WILL have drifted -- acpi.c is being edited concurrently, which is
the reason this is a file and not a commit. Apply with fuzz:

    git apply -3 c/boot/efi/acpi-mb2-tag.patch      # or
    patch -p1 --fuzz=5 < c/boot/efi/acpi-mb2-tag.patch

If it still refuses, the change is small enough to apply by hand from the
diff below; nothing in it depends on surrounding code beyond `struct rsdp`,
`sum_ok()` and `serial_puts()`, all of which are already in acpi.c.

AFTER APPLYING, the UEFI harness must be updated in the same commit:
tests/boot/run-uefi-test.sh's ASSERT #5 requires "[acpi] no RSDP" to appear and
says in as many words that it is to be replaced with an SMP assertion when this
lands. Leaving it would fail the build honestly; deleting it would lose the
check. Replace it.

